The strongest possible Pāṭala endgames

    The hard reference layer for historical knowledge. Start Sanskrit, then Pāli/Tibetan, Greek/Latin, Arabic and other documentary traditions. Agents query Pāṭala not because Pāṭala's model is smarter but because it contains the best accumulated state of identities, passages, translations, claims, disagreements and provenance.

    The operating system for philosophy. Instead of philosophy being millions of disconnected PDFs, build philosopher → work → passage → proposition → argument → objection → reply → school/tradition → later influence. Agents could ask not merely “What did Nāgārjuna say?” but “Show every major argument against substantial universals across Nyāya, Buddhist apoha theory, Plato and contemporary analytic metaphysics, preserving differences in epistemic regime.”

    The Scholar OS. Scholarship becomes continuously compiled rather than paper-shaped. Papers remain publications, but Pāṭala extracts their assertions, evidence and disagreements into live state. Scholars spend less time repeating literature reviews and more time resolving high-information questions. Their review/calibration history becomes a genuine professional asset.

    The education compiler we designed. Once the graph actually knows propositions, arguments, cruxes, rival interpretations and common misconceptions, lessons are no longer generic generated explanations. The system can generate assessments where the only way to choose the correct answer is to demonstrate understanding of the argument. Learner failures become observations that improve both teaching and potentially flag problems upstream.

    A reality market. Agents exhaust cheap reasoning, produce a precise RealityRequest, and humans/institutions are compensated for scarce observations: inspect this folio, resolve this reading, identify this object, verify this archival fact. Eventually the same mechanism generalizes to field observations and experiments.

    A full active-science OS. Question → hypotheses → enormous inference → discriminating predictions → experimental obligation → lab/robot/human executes → observation returns → belief graph updates. At that point Sanskrit was simply the unusually difficult first domain that taught the system how to distinguish inference from reality.

The sixth is the enormous endgame. But you don't need to build it to make the first three valuable.
